[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) merged every date report into one ticket, because their subject lines
matched ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). Read by cause, the reports
describe three different bugs. Each now has its own ticket with the number of reports it covers.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 was not one known issue with one fix. Engineering closed it on 5 October with the parser
fixed and the booking-v3 form retired. The one-day move is still open.
</Callout>

## The split

<SplitTable ticket={4471} repo="mjura/what-it-shows" issues={{ parser: 1, hint: 2, 'one-day': 3 }} />

## Where it stands

From engineering's update when #4471 closed on 5 October:

- **`parser`**: fixed, `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Ticket closed as completed.
- **`hint`**: won't fix, the booking-v3 form is retired. Ticket closed as not planned.
- **`one-day`**: not in the update. Neither the parser fix nor the form retirement touches time
  zones, so its ticket stays open.

## What each one is

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, so a day-first tenant's dates come
  out with day and month swapped. App, email and booking-v2 forms, every tenant on file.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the
  hint says, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. Only the booking-v3 form, so fixing
  the parser does not fix it.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, not a swap. One meeting showed as Tuesday in Lisbon and
  Monday in Toronto: a time zone bug. The desk had already read these as a one-day move
  (`desk-notes.json`). They stay counted under the parser by tenant, as the desk counts, and have
  their own ticket too.

## How it was counted

- The export pages overlap, so the same report can appear twice. Counted once by id.
- Counted the desk's way: every report under the cause its tenant gives it, so the causes add up
  to all reports merged into #4471.
- Cause comes from the tenant, its locale and its booking form (`causes.ts`), not from intake's tag.
  The tag is a guess from the reporter's browser, and it often disagrees with the tenant.
- Every body with two dates was checked: the `parser` and `hint` reports swap day and month; none
  of them is a one-day move.

## Lessons

- A matching subject line says nothing about cause. Count by cause before marking a big ticket known.
- Read the bodies of a sample before the ticket is closed as one issue.

